WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:06.180
Hi, my name is Kyle Mcllhone and I’m from
Canada.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:12.060
So I first started studying acting at Dawson
College and I did that for two years.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.590
And after that I worked from three years in
Montreal, mostly in theater.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:20.950
And then I moved here to New York to enroll
at The Neighborhood Playhouse and I graduated

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.910
in 2015.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.410
And since then I’ve been working here.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:30.010
And this year Karan [Choudhary] and I produced
NEW YORK’D and it’s doing very well in

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:32.230
film festivals and we’re very proud of it.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:33.230
[Scene to NEW YORK’D movie]

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:35.010
[Text appears and delivery man shows Karan
the phone]

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:36.789
“Hey, look…my boss” Kyle Mcllhone as
The Delivery Guy

00:00:36.789 --> 00:00:37.789
“Alright, I’ve also got to go.”

00:00:37.789 --> 00:00:38.789
Replies Sean

00:00:38.789 --> 00:00:40.500
“Just pay me and deal with your own personal
problems later, huh?”

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.670
“My personal problems?

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:43.899
You’re the one who is causing these personal
problems.”

00:00:43.899 --> 00:00:44.899
“What?

00:00:44.899 --> 00:00:46.989
Are you blaming me for getting yourself locked
out of your apartment?”

00:00:46.989 --> 00:00:48.089
“You are the delivery guy.

00:00:48.089 --> 00:00:49.090
You’ve got one job to do.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.230
If you had done your job like you are supposed
to.”

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.559
“If you’d cook your own food like you’re
supposed to…”

00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:55.819
“You make a living because of people like
me.”

00:00:55.819 --> 00:00:58.729
“And you get to enjoy hot food in bed because
of people like me.”

00:00:58.729 --> 00:00:59.729
“Yeah…where is it?

00:00:59.729 --> 00:01:00.729
Where is my hot food?”

00:01:00.729 --> 00:01:04.500
“I just want to get my money and get out
of here.”

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.940
The biggest problem for me has been the business
of acting.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:12.480
I got into acting because I was passionate
about acting.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:17.940
But then when you choose to make it your career,
you have to buy into the business of acting

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:24.010
which an be better headshots, really going
somewhere where you have to pay a lot of money

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.010
for that.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:28.060
Getting a reel, paying someone for a reel.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:34.520
Then agents, managers, networking with them,
going to classes to meet casting directors,

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:35.520
so many more.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.050
And social media is a big one.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:45.190
And that clashed along with my passion because
I’m not really that kind of person who is

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:48.400
good at that.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:53.670
So how I’ve been dealing with this problem
is I’ve been dealing with my own projects

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:58.250
because that way I can get my own reel, I’m
learning, I have something to talk about,

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:04.660
I am doing something, I’m not waiting by
a phone, I’m active.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.820
As an actor you want to keep yourself tuned.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:12.879
You want to be always acting more and more.

00:02:12.879 --> 00:02:17.079
And when you start off you think you’re
going to do that through actually auditioning

00:02:17.079 --> 00:02:21.190
and doing stuff that you will be paid for,
you know?

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:23.139
Not always the case.

00:02:23.139 --> 00:02:28.389
And so you can get in the rhythm of (I think
someone once said) An Auditioning Actor.

00:02:28.389 --> 00:02:32.819
Like someone who is good at auditions but
when it comes to a full script, it’s actually

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:35.459
foreign to them because they haven’t done
it for awhile.

00:02:35.459 --> 00:02:42.620
I’ve been fortunate I think because something
I consciously do is to alway try and act so

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:50.249
I’ve done a lot of plays for free which
I’m very grateful for in the end because

00:02:50.249 --> 00:02:51.959
it kept me fine tuned.

00:02:51.959 --> 00:02:58.000
I’m working with people who have worked
longer in the business.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:04.590
I’m learning and not only that I’m being
challenged with a new role and all of that.

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:07.879
And that I think his just good for my mind,
I think it keeps me happier.

00:03:07.879 --> 00:03:13.609
Thank you so much for watching this interview
and my question would be to you guys if any

00:03:13.609 --> 00:03:18.049
of you who are actors or producers, what problems
you come into?

00:03:18.049 --> 00:03:20.709
What problems have you deal with the business
and how have you learned?

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:21.549
Thank you!

